editorial:
## Method and reasoning
Store the last position of each letter. When adding a repeated letter, move the left boundary just after its previous occurrence if that occurrence is inside the window. The boundary never moves backward.

Before adding a character the window is unique. The only possible conflict involves the newly added character. Removing through its previous position is therefore sufficient and removes the fewest characters possible. Taking the maximum of these longest valid suffixes covers every endpoint.

## Walk through the sample
In abbaef, the second b forces the left boundary past the first b. At the next a, its previous position lies outside the window, so the boundary stays put. The final window baef has length 4.

## Complexity
Time O(n) and O(1) auxiliary space.

## Reference implementation
~~~
#include <bits/stdc++.h>
using namespace std;

int main() {
    string s;
    cin >> s;
    array<int, 26> last;
    last.fill(-1);
    int left = 0, best = 0;
    for (int r = 0; r < int(s.size()); ++r) {
        int c = s[r] - 'a';
        left = max(left, last[c] + 1);
        last[c] = r;
        best = max(best, r - left + 1);
    }
    cout << best << '\n';
}
~~~
